In Java, class loaders are responsible for dynamically loading classes at runtime. There are several types of class loaders, each with a specific purpose in the class-loading hierarchy.

🔄 Types of Class Loaders in Java
1. Bootstrap ClassLoader
Loads: Core Java classes (java.lang.*, java.util.*, etc.) from the $JAVA_HOME/jre/lib directory.

Part of JVM and written in native code (not Java).

Parent of all other class loaders.

2. Extension ClassLoader (Platform ClassLoader in Java 9+)
Loads: Classes from the $JAVA_HOME/lib/ext/ directory or specified by the java.ext.dirs system property.

Child of Bootstrap ClassLoader.

From Java 9 onward, replaced by Platform ClassLoader, which loads classes from platform modules.

3. System ClassLoader (Application ClassLoader)
Loads: Classes from the classpath, typically the -cp or CLASSPATH environment variable.

Child of Extension/Platform ClassLoader.

Most of your application classes are loaded by this loader.

🧰 Custom Class Loaders
4. User-defined (Custom) ClassLoader
Created by extending java.lang.ClassLoader.

Used for:

Custom class loading policies

Loading encrypted or remote classes

Dynamic reloading in frameworks (e.g., OSGi, Tomcat, Spring Boot DevTools)

🔁 ClassLoader Hierarchy (Parent Delegation Model)

Bootstrap
   ↑
Extension / Platform
   ↑
System (Application)
   ↑
Custom ClassLoader (if defined)

Each class loader delegates class loading to its parent first, and only tries to load the class itself if the parent cannot find it (known as the parent delegation model).